AB  - Human resource allocation challenges in developing regions necessitate geographic strategies that integrate spatial analysis with artificial intelligence (AI). This study examines employment-population relationships across 495 sub-districts in Bangladesh utilizing Geographically Weighted Regression, machine learning, and Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI). An analysis of census data from 2001 to 2011 demonstrated that administrative employment contributed most significantly (32%) to population dynamics, followed by the agricultural sector (28%) and the industrial sector (20%). Forest-based regression models (R2 = 0.902) surpassed traditional regression models (R2 = 0.88) in predictive accuracy. Advanced GeoAI techniques yielded additional insights beyond conventional methodologies, including Variational Autoencoders that identified six employment pattern clusters with spatial coherence, thereby unveiling latent structures obscured to traditional approaches. Graph Neural Networks captured spatial spillover effects through modeling neighborhood dependencies (R2 = 0.216), offering a complementary perspective on inter-regional demographic linkages. SHapley Additive exPlanations analysis quantified sector contributions, identifying Household Working (importance: 3051), Agricultural Working (2,469), and Public Administration (2,384) as the most influential predictors. The capital region experienced the highest population increase (42%), with 54 sub-districts exhibiting clusters of administrative employment. Agricultural employment demonstrated significant spatial correlation (Moran's I = 0.250, p < 0.001) in rural areas. The integrated framework identified 67 sub-districts requiring diversification of employment, 34 suitable for administrative expansion, and 28 needing agricultural support. Principal Component Analysis revealed four components accounting for 78% of employment variance. These findings demonstrate how spatially explicit AI techniques can complement traditional methods in addressing human resource challenges via geographic approaches. This approach provides quantitative evidence and interpretable insights, enabling policymakers to mitigate regional disparities and manage population distribution through evidence-based spatial resource strategies. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
