TI  - Heterogeneity-aware deep learning for spatiotemporal data: a methodological review
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Journal of Geographical Information Science
DO  - 10.1080/13658816.2026.2714043
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046678990?origin=resultslist
AD  - State Key Laboratory of Resources and Environmental Information System, Institute of Geographic Sciences and Natural Resources Research, CAS, Beijing, China
AD  - University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, China
AD  - The Academy of Digital China, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou, China
AD  - Jiangsu Center for Collaborative Innovation in Geographical Information Resource Development and Application, Nanjing, China
AB  - Spatiotemporal heterogeneity is an important property of geographic phenomena and a challenge for moving deep learning from generic data fitting toward geographic knowledge-constrained modeling. Although common implementations of convolution, graph propagation, and attention capture spatiotemporal dependencies, their parameter-sharing mechanisms and static inductive biases often assume global stationarity, hindering adaptation when variable distributions and relationships vary across space and time. Existing heterogeneity-aware designs remain fragmented across tasks, data forms, and architectures, lacking a synthesis of explicit heterogeneity modeling mechanisms. This review presents a mechanism-level framework centered on variable and relational heterogeneity. By integrating stratified and local organizational structures, the framework organizes existing methods around three stages: feature representation, model parameterization, and objective optimization. It identifies six core strategies: stratified and local feature representation, stratified and local parameterization, and feature self-supervised and parameter regularization constraints. We delineated the boundary between explicit heterogeneity-aware modeling and generic spatiotemporal context encoding, and analyzed the theoretical basis, mechanisms, applicability, and limitations of each strategy. We further discussed challenges in coupled heterogeneity modeling, spatiotemporal transferability, interpretability, computational complexity, uncertainty quantification, and spatiotemporal foundation models. This review provides a methodological reference for understanding how deep learning models represent, adapt to, and constrain spatiotemporal heterogeneity in GeoAI. © 2026 Informa UK Limited, trading as Taylor & Francis Group.
