AD  - Yildiz Technical University, Esenler, Istanbul, 34210, Turkey
AB  - Control point (CP) selection is a crucial preprocessing step in network alignment workflows in GIS and spatial data integration. CP-based registration commonly depends on selecting corresponding intersections between a source network and a target network, after which a geometric transformation such as similarity, affine, projective, or locally adaptive warping can be estimated. This paper evaluates GPT-5.2 “thinking” mode, a multimodal Large Language Model (LLM) for CP detection and correspondence establishment between two network inputs that represent the same geographic extent. We analyze twelve experimental runs organized by three factors: input modality as rasterized network imagery versus vector networks, availability of explicit intersection node markers and identifier labels versus line-only inputs, and distortion level of the target network including regular affine-like distortions, globally irregular distortions, and local distortions. The results indicate that regular affine-like deformations enable strong LLM-assisted CP detection, particularly when nodes are provided and the raster/vector representation is chosen appropriately. This advantage diminishes under irregular distortions (global or local), where achieving consistently good performance becomes more difficult in vector-based settings. Finally, when deformation exists by localized detail-level differences, neither raster nor vector yields reliable results, making local distortions a modality-agnostic limitation for the current LLM-assisted CP detection setup. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.
