AB  - This paper examines how probabilistic species distribution modelling using Maximum Entropy (MaxEnt), implemented within a geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI)-based workflow, can be employed as a design support tool within landscape architectural practice in semi-arid environments. Applied within the context of a giga-scale development project in Saudi Arabia, the approach supported early-stage plant selection. Using concatenated botanical survey records, 28 plant species were modelled against a suite of 15 abiotic environmental variables following data cleaning, minimum occurrence thresholds (≥10 records), multicollinearity screening using variance inflation factors, and expert-led verification. The resulting outputs generated continuous, species-specific probability surfaces that revealed regional patterns of environmental association. The paper argues that while GeoAI-based workflows using MaxEnt offer valuable insights into regional environmental patterns, they systematically underrepresent biotic interactions and facilitation processes that govern species establishment within designed landscapes. Accordingly, model outputs are most effective when positioned as provisional design instruments within iterative digital landscape architecture workflows that integrate GeoAI-enabled modelling with field observation and design judgement, rather than being treated as predictive ecological truths. © Wichmann Verlag,
