AD  - Department of Social Studies (Geography), Ewha Womans University, Seoul, South Korea
AB  - Urban street trees provide various environmental benefits, but they are continuously exposed to urban pollutants which may lead to reduced vitality. Most previous studies have focused on analyzing vegetation vitality in forests and rural areas due to the complex environmental characteristics of urban settings. Accordingly, this study aims to analyze the vitality of individual street trees in urban areas by using high-resolution aerial and satellite imagery with deep learning models. Training data for street tree detection was generated using orthorectified aerial imagery. Deep learning models such as U-Net, Mask2former, and DeepLabV3 were applied for tree detection, with U-Net demonstrating the best performance in identifying individual street trees. NDVI and NDMI were computed and integrated to derive a vitality index. The results revealed that street trees planted along roads exhibited lower vitality compared to those located near apartment complexes, walkways, and parks. These findings suggest the influence of urban planting environments on tree vitality. By enabling precise vitality analysis of individual trees, this study offers a practical foundation for large-scale monitoring and improved management of urban green spaces. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
