May 2025, 200533. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsinno.2025.200533 Funding Statement: This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Ethical Statement: This research was based on a questionnaire survey involving human participants. At the time of data collection, no institutional ethics committee had yet been established at my university. Therefore, no formal ethics approval or reference number was required. The study nevertheless respected the standard ethical principles for non-interventional survey research. Participants were clearly informed, in the introductory message accompanying the questionnaire, about the purpose of the study and how the data would be used. Participation was voluntary, and no personal data or identifying information were collected. Informed Consent Statement: Informed consent was obtained from all participants. The introductory message provided participants with clear information about the study and the intended use of their responses, and consent was implied through their voluntary completion of the questionnaire.11. “Examining the impact of digital transformation on digital product innovation performance in banking industry through the integration of resource-based view and dynamic capabilities”. Journal of Strategy & Innovation, Volume 36, Issue 1, May 2025, 200540. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsinno.2025.200540 Ethical Statement: This study received ethical clearance from the STMIK Bandung Ethics Committee under approval number 023/EC/X/2024, issued on October 12, 2024. Informed Consent Statement: Prior to data collection, all participants provided written informed consent to participate in the study. Participants were informed about the research objectives, the voluntary nature of participation, the confidentiality safeguards in place, and their right to withdraw from the study at any time without consequence. In addition, because the study reports participants' organizational positions, separate written informed consent for the publication of non-identifiable professional details was obtained. All identifying information has been anonymized to protect individual and institutional confidentiality, and no personal names or sensitive institutional data are disclosed.12. “ChatGPT's infusion advantage: The moderating role of strategic agility”. Journal of Strategy & Innovation, Volume 36, Issue 1, May 2025, 200541. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsinno.2025.200541 Ethical Statement: This study was conducted using an anonymous questionnaire survey. Participation was voluntary, and respondents were informed of the purpose of the research before completing the questionnaire. No personally identifiable information was collected. According to institutional and national guidelines for social science research, formal ethical approval was not required. Informed Consent Statement: Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to their participation in the survey.13. “Analysing spatial heterogeneity of business opportunity and competition in refurbished electronics market: A geospatial AI study”. Journal of Strategy & Innovation, Volume 37, Issue 1, May 2026, 200571. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsinno.2026.200571 Conflict of Interest: The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Funding Statement: This research was partially supported by the Center for Financial Studies (CFS), Management Development Institute Gurgaon, India. The funder had no role in the study design, data collection, analysis, interpretation of data, or in the writing of the manuscript. Ethical Statement: This study did not involve human participants, human data, or human tissue. The research was based entirely on secondary/publicly available data and geospatial analysis. Therefore, ethical approval was not required. Informed Consent Statement: Not applicable. This study did not involve human participants.14. “Artificial intelligence-driven healthcare supply chain resilience: The mediating effect of organisational transparency”. Journal of Strategy & Innovation, Volume 37, Issue 1, May 2026, 200572. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jsinno.2026.200572 Conflict of Interest: The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Funding Statement: This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Ethical Statement: Name of the ethics committee – University Research Ethics Committees (UREC) - Liverpool John Moores University. Approval number - UREC approval reference number: 23/LBS/015. Informed Consent Statement: I confirm that informed consent was obtained from all participants. © 2026
