AB  - Water resources are critically important for ecological, social, and economic systems. Accurate extraction of water bodies is essential for estimating the spatial extent of water resources and mitigating water-related disasters. Single-modal remote sensing images are often insufficient for accurate water body extraction due to limitations in spectral information, adverse weather conditions, and speckle noise. Furthermore, state-of-the-art deep learning models may be limited by data extensibility, feature transferability, model scalability, and task generalizability. To address these challenges, this study presents an integrated GeoAI framework that leverages foundation models for accurate and efficient water body extraction from multi-modal remote sensing images. The proposed framework includes a data augmentation module specifically designed for optical and Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) images, along with extraction modules enhanced by three popular types of foundation models. Experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of the foundation models and the benefits of fusing optical and SAR remote sensing images, significantly improving water extraction performance compared to single-modal approaches. © 2026 SPIE.
