AB  - The problem of climate change requires more sophisticated methods of analysis to learn how to comprehend the effects of climate change on the environment, society, and economy. This review identifies the latest advances in the field of artificial intelligence (AI), remote sensing and hybrid modelling frameworks to be used in evaluating risks associated with climate. Approaches which are time-series forecasting, generative models, or physics-informed machine learning are considered as key ones. The techniques are used in areas like water resource, extreme weather (i.e., floods, GLOFs, cyclones), agriculture and food security, urban resilience of infrastructure, and socio-economic systems. Large-scale geospatial data processing is facilitated by sites such as Google Earth Engine to carry out such analyses. Nevertheless, even with the substantial progress, there are still some obstacles to deal with such as the gaps in data, rough spatial resolution, insufficient ground-truth data, and the loss of transparency regarding AI black-box models. The proposed framework can support climate adaptation planning and policy decisions in vulnerable regions. © 2026 IEEE.
