AB  - Mobility prediction is a fundamental task for location-based services and traffic management. However, state-of-the-art models often rely on computationally expensive architectures such as Transformers, making them unsuitable for resource-constrained edge environments. This study investigates a lightweight approach that combines structured model slimming with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). First, we apply structured slimming using width multipliers to a recurrent neural network backbone, reducing its parameter count by approximately 35%. To compensate for the reduced capacity and improve predictive accuracy, we introduce a non-parametric retrieval mechanism that augments the model's predictions with relevant historical trajectory contexts at inference time. Experiments on the GeoLife dataset show that this hybrid approach not only recovers the accuracy lost due to slimming but also surpasses the original uncompressed baseline, reducing the Horizon-5 error from 63.48, m to 59.84, m (an improvement of approximately 5.73%). Furthermore, the retrieval-augmented model exhibits negligible performance degradation under simulated GPS dropout conditions, demonstrating greater robustness to signal loss compared with the baseline model. © 2026 IEEE.
