AB  - Volunteered geographic information (VGI) provides valuable geospatial cues for disaster management. However, existing geolocalization methods often overlook the potential of space-aerial-ground integrated views and rely on street view imagery (SVI) as the ground-level view, while underutilizing VGI that better reflects real-world conditions. In this article, we propose SAGINGeo, a cross-view geolocalization framework that improves VGI geolocalization by first integrating panoramic SVI and unmanned aerial vehicle (UAV) imagery as intermediates to remote sensing imagery (RSI). Powered by a ViT-based unified multiview training scheme, SAGINGeo jointly models spatial information from space, aerial, and ground views to learn a shared representation. An anchor-guided reranking strategy is optionally applied to refine retrieval based on semantic similarity. To evaluate our framework, we constructed SAGINDisaster, the first multidisaster cross-view dataset integrating VGI, SVI, UAV, and RSI across four disaster types, comprising 2080 data groups, 118 560 images, and 2080 simulated UAV videos. Experiments show that SAGINGeo consistently outperforms the baselines with or without reranking assistance. With a single informative SVI and UAV image and an 8:2 data split, SAGINGeo increases Recall@1, Recall@5, Recall@10, and Recall@1% to 48.56%, 78.85%, 87.26%, and 76.20%, respectively. This study highlights the potential of combining multiview training to support disaster response and expand GeoAI applications. The code and dataset will be released upon acceptance.  © 2026 IEEE.
