AB  - Accurate mapping of floriculture is essential for efficient agricultural management and sustainable practices in the flower industry. However, conventional remote sensing techniques are not sufficient for mapping floriculture in diverse, heterogenous landscape. This study explores the potentiality of machine learning and deep learning models for identification of floriculture areas, specifically, rose farms integrating with high-resolution multispectral and hyperspectral datasets. The PlanetScope SuperDove and PRISMA data were employed independently and as fused datasets, incorporating multiple vegetation indices, including NDVI, SAVI, OSAVI, and NDWI, to enhance model accuracy. Spectral signatures of rose and marigold flowers along with GPS based sample points were collected during the field survey. The classification models evaluated in this study included Random Forest (RF), Support Vector Machine (SVM), Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), and Deep Neural Network (DNN), each assessed for their ability to differentiate various features within complex, heterogeneous floriculture farms. This study revealed that the fusion of high spectral and spatial resolution data significantly improves classification accuracy. Deep learning model, especially when integrated with fused datasets, achieved notable performance metrics, an overall accuracy of 83% and a Kappa coefficient of 78% surpassing traditional machine learning approaches. This model demonstrated a higher recall of 0.86 and an F1-score of 0.83, indicating its superior ability to accurately distinguish rose farms from other crops with subtle spectral differences. Integration of high resolution multi-spectral data with the PRISMA hyperspectral image enhanced the potentiality of GeoAI based models. This research would be helpful for policy intervention, resource allocation and environmental management strategies, facilitating more efficient agricultural practices and thereby, promoting sustainable practices. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
