AD  - Singapore Institute Of Technology, Power & Energy Lab, Engineering Cluster, Singapore
AD  - Graphic Era Hill University, Department Of Cse, Dehradun, India
AB  - The implementation of solar energy infrastructure in India requires that special attention is paid to local climate, geographic, and environmental factors, as the country is highly diverse in the region. The proposed work is based on the prior work carried out by undertaking a state wise analysis to determine the best location to be used to install the solar panels. Geo-spatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) framework was created through coordinating high resolution climatic parameters, topology, and land-use data. As part of the initial stages of this study, a number of machine learning algorithms such as Xtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost), Random Forest (RF), Gradient Boosting, k-nearest Neighbours (kNN), Logistic regression, and a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) were coded and tested to provide an approximation of the suitability of solar energy in various regions of India. Based on the Comparative analysis, RF and XGBoost had the best predictive performance. The results of MLP and kNN were similar, but the accuracy of Logistic regression and Gradient Boosting was also stable but lower in comparison. In order to improve the quality of the state-level evaluation, the top performing algorithms RF and XGBoost were re-trained on larger resolution datasets. The redefined models had a prediction accuracy of 95 and 94 respectively. The outcome shows that there is a huge spatial disparity in solar energy potential, which is useful as a guide in regional-specific planning and deployment approach. Moreover, the results show that the ensemble learning methods are suitable in geographical heterogeneity, reinforcing the validity of the suggested GeoAI founded framework as a policy-supporting system, energy planner, and urban developer.  © 2026 IEEE.
