AB  - In response to the 'geometric hallucinations' of large language models in spatial topological reasoning, this paper introduces Freksa's conceptual neighborhood theory and a topology distance metric to conduct a systematic diagnosis at the level of cognitive mechanisms. Comparative experiments on a balanced test set reveal that LLMs generally face a bottleneck in 'spatial semantic resolution,' and that chain-of-thought strategies can induce a pronounced 'capability polarization effect.' The study confirms that these models primarily learn fuzzy distributions of topological semantics rather than precise computational rules. On this basis, we propose a 'neuro-symbolic collaboration' architecture grounded in resolution boundaries, providing a new pathway toward building trustworthy GeoAI.  © 2026 IEEE.
