AB  - The majority of digital map production relies on manual labor, resulting in substantial time and cost expenditures, and exhibits significant variations in quality depending on the operator's proficiency. To overcome these challenges, this study proposed a method for efficiently generating digital maps of buildings by applying deep learning models for object detection and segmentation, along with image processing techniques, to ortho-images. Specifically, building objects within the ortho-images were identified using a YOLO (You Only Look Once) OBB (Oriented Bounding Box) detection model, and based on the detected buildings, complex prompts were generated utilizing image processing techniques. The generated complex prompts were applied to a SAM (Segment Anything Model) together with the ortho-images to precisely segment building areas. Subsequently, additional image processing was performed to remove errors and produce a digital map. To validate the proposed methodology, a quality inspection was conducted on a randomly selected sample comprising 30% of the generated digital maps. The error rate of excess errors was about 7.1%, and that of omission errors about 10.7%. Furthermore, the results of the depiction error analysis revealed an average Hausdorff distance of approximately 2.72 pixels; considering the 1:5,000 scale, this corresponds to a real-world distance error of approximately 1.36 meters, demonstrating an overall satisfactory level of agreement. © 2026, Korean Society of Surveying Geodesy Photogrammetry and Cartography. All rights reserved.
