AB  - With the increasing impacts of climate change, natural hazards, aging infrastructure, and urbanization, bridge asset management demands more sophisticated geospatial data-driven solutions. This chapter emphasizes the critical role of geospatial artificial intelligence (AI) (GeoAI) technologies in modernizing and optimizing bridge management by enabling centralized integration and visualization of diverse bridge data. Remote sensing, Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), and digital twin technologies provide multi-scale insights into bridge and environmental conditions. High-resolution imagery and drone data detect early structural changes, while digital twins enable real-time monitoring and scenario simulations. Combined with AI-driven analytics, these tools reveal predictive patterns and risk factors, offering a unified view of bridge health. This integrated geospatial approach supports predictive maintenance, informed decision-making, and sustainable lifecycle management of critical infrastructure. GeoAI-driven approaches shift bridge management from a traditionally reactive paradigm to a proactive, intelligence-driven discipline, leading to optimized maintenance scheduling, efficient resource allocation, extended asset lifespans, and enhanced safety, resilience, and sustainability of bridge networks. © 2026 CRC Press.
