AD  - University of Wisconsin–Madison, United States
AD  - Cornell University, United States
AB  - Accurate and reliable assessment of driving risks enhances road safety and supports efficient urban transportation management. This type of work utilizes diverse data sources and evaluates driving risks from multiple aspects. However, it remains challenging due to the complexity of heterogeneous data and the difficulty in interpreting varied results. This study introduces a novel GeoAI framework that employs Vision-Language Models (VLMs) with a knowledge-guided Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system to better estimate and evaluate driving risk from the geospatial environment perspective. Specifically, the study proposes a systematic prompt design framework that explicitly instructs VLMs for precise reasoning in driving risk scenarios. The expert knowledge-guided RAG system significantly improves the interpretability and accuracy of the initial VLM-generated results (with about 17% average improvement in few-shot learning). Furthermore, a dual evaluation incorporating both objective performance metrics and subjective human rankings is applied to evaluate the results, revealing the robustness and practicality of our framework. We demonstrate that this framework provides an accurate identification and evaluation of driving risk under the real-world geospatial environment context. This research contributes to the advancements of traffic safety studies with VLMs and RAG and offers insights into geospatial environment reasoning tasks and multimodal GeoAI applications. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)
