AD  - Key Laboratory of Comprehensive Observation of Polar Environment (Sun Yat-Sen University), Ministry of Education, Zhuhai, China
AB  - We present a challenging change detection problem in vector building footprints, which is critical in updating vector databases for sustained urban analysis and different from remote sensing change detection. Existing approaches struggle to distinguish real changes from pseudochanges caused by levels of detail, cartographic displacement, positional accuracy, and map generalization in vector data. To bridge this gap and to address the limitations of end-to-end models in our task, we propose a multistage workflow that organizes deep models in a hierarchical structure. Specifically, we device domain-specific attention mechanisms that focus on spatial subregions, which helps the workflow to learn multiscale concepts (e.g., discrepancy and displacement) key to human reasoning for change detection. Our experiments show that by learning intermediate concepts, our workflow significantly outperformed the end-to-end models, as well as a conventional (rule-based) algorithm, by largely reducing pseudochange rates from over 40% to 16.7%. The boost from the workflow is generally observed from traditional CNNs to modern architectures such as Transformers and ConvNeXt, which confirms that the multistage workflow is more effective in vector-based change detection with small data. The code is open sourced at https://github.com/WHU-X-Lab/BCDWorkflow. © 2025 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
