AB  - Air pollution exposure during daily commuting poses significant health risks, yet most route-planning studies focus on PM₂.₅ and rely on coarse spatial interpolation methods. This study advances exposure-aware mobility by developing a high-resolution geospatial artificial intelligence (Geo-AI) framework for Kaohsiung City, Taiwan. This framework models nitrogen dioxide (NO₂), a traffic-related pollutant with sharp spatial gradients, and integrates it into an optimized route-planning system. A Light Gradient Boosting Machine Regression (LGBMR) model was trained on continuous monitoring data from December 2021 to February 2024. The model achieved robust predictive performance (R² = 0.87), enabling reliable estimates of pollutant concentrations at the street segment scale. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis revealed that chemical-industry density, waste-treatment facility density, and road network density were among the most influential predictors of elevated NO₂. By combining 10 × 10 m NO₂ concentration data from the model and a detailed road network database, we simulated 3000 randomized commuting routes and compared distance-optimized and exposure-optimized routing scenarios. On average, low-exposure routing reduced NO₂ exposure by 2.04%, while 259 routes achieved reductions of over 5%. In some cases, the reduction reached 39.70%. A representative 365-day commuting simulation was conducted for the entire year of 2024. It projected an annual cumulative reduction in exposure of 9.02 × 10⁵ ppb·s for a typical commuter using optimized routes. These results underscore the feasibility and public health potential of integrating high-resolution pollutant mapping with route optimization, enabling personalized, exposure-conscious mobility. It offers a scalable pathway for urban planners and policymakers to mitigate long-term health risks, especially in densely populated Asian cities with high motor vehicle use. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
