AD  - School of Public Administration, Chongqing University, Chongqing, 400044, China
AB  - Global warming and intensified human activities have led to a continued deterioration of the urban thermal environment, posing a major challenge to sustainable urban development. Intra-urban heat islands (IUHIs), a key manifestation of extreme heat events, are particularly prominent in metropolis. However, existing methods often fail to detect subtle variations in land surface temperature (LST) in complex environments, limiting our understanding of the spatial patterns and driving mechanisms of IUHIs. This study focuses on Chongqing, a representative mountainous city. Constructing a GeoAI-based LST downscaling approach, produced 10 m resolution LST data (R² = 0.86). Results indicate that the summer of 2024 recorded the highest mean LST over the past decade (41.39 ℃), characterized by marked spatial differentiation, and distinct thermal contrasts. IUHIs exhibited a multi-centered clustered distribution closely aligned with industrial areas, which accounted for 48.21 % of identified hotspots—highlighting industrial as the main culprit behind urban heat. Furthermore, its internal thermal environment showed pronounced spatial heterogeneity due to geomorphic variation. The driving mechanisms of IUHIs are revealed as a complex system structured along an altitudinal gradient, shaped by nonlinear interactions among topographic, environmental, and social factors. This study underscores the importance of regulating IUHIs to mitigate urban heat, advocating targeted cooling strategies for industrial areas. The findings offer both valuable data and novel insights, providing a scientific foundation for high-temperature mitigation in other high-density cities, with significant practical implications for urban renewal and green development. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
