AB  - Rapid urbanization and population aging are transforming landscapes in metropolitan suburbs, where changes in courtyard serve as a key spatial response by farming households to shifting conditions. Existing research lacks detailed detection methods and large-scale dynamic monitoring techniques for courtyard types, limiting understanding of spatial variation patterns and their driving forces. This study focuses on 2568 villages in Beijing's suburbs, developing a Geo-courtyard detection method based on an improved YOLOv11 model and Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) technology. We achieved, for the first time, high-precision, large-scale automatic detection and measurement of courtyard types, including Courtyard Dwellings (CD), Covered Courtyards (CC), and Detached Courtyards (DC), along with their spatiotemporal changes. The village-scale accuracy reached 94.29% for courtyard detection and 92.08% for classification of detected courtyards, with an F1 score of 0.922. Our findings reveal that CC increased by 36.45% from 2010 to 2020, forming polycentric clustering patterns primarily driven by out-migration and increased precipitation. DC grew by 15.14%, concentrated mainly in shallow mountain transition zones, driven by rental demand from in-migrating populations and the spatial allocation needs of extended families. Geographically and Temporally Weighted Regression (GTWR) and in-depth interviews revealed spatial heterogeneity in driving factors. The study establishes a multi-adaptive courtyard differentiation pattern under institutional constraints in metropolitan areas, providing valuable data and scientific evidence for detailed landscape planning and sustainable governance in metropolitan suburbs. © 2026 The Authors.
