TI  - A Secure and Privacy-Preserving Hybrid GeoAI Framework for MSME Business Opportunity Mapping in South Jakarta
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Enterprise and Industrial System, ICOEINS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICOEINS69996.2026.11605819
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046420658?origin=resultslist
AD  - Politeknik Siber dan Sandi Negara, Cryptographic Engineering Program, Bogor, Indonesia
AD  - Politeknik Siber dan Sandi Negara, Department of Cryptographic Engineering, Bogor, Indonesia
AB  - As the utilization of geospatial data and digital economic indicators in machine learning-based analysis grows, safeguarding data security, privacy, and information integrity has become a critical prerequisite for ensuring the reliability of regional economic decision-making. Micro, Small, and Medium Enterprises (MSMEs), for instance, play a pivotal role in regional economic resilience, yet they continue to face significant hurdles in pinpointing optimal business locations, where location-related failures contribute to approximately 60% of new business closures. Amidst escalating competitiveness driven by sustainable networks and digital financial inclusion, strategic mapping is not only hindered by the scarcity of high-resolution (fine-grained) socioeconomic data at the sub-district level but also by inherent risks to data security and privacy that can compromise the dependability of machine learning-driven analytics. This study proposes a hybrid Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) framework designed to identify "Blue Ocean"areas characterized by high market demand and low competition while explicitly incorporating data security, privacy, and integrity into the machine learning pipeline. The proposed framework integrates K-Means clustering and multi-criteria weighted scoring through five primary phases: (1) multi-layer data acquisition combining official statistics and digital economic indicators; (2) implementation of spatial data aggregation mechanisms as a privacy-preserving measure to mitigate information leakage risks; (3) market segmentation based on K-Means clustering; (4) multi-criteria weighted scoring utilizing distributional adjustment for location prioritization; and (5) temporal backtesting validation using 2023-2025 data alongside linear regression-based population prediction. To ensure the robustness of the GeoAI analysis, geospatial data integrity from various sources is rigorously factored into the preprocessing workflow prior to the execution of machine learning algorithms. The system is evaluated across 65 sub-districts (kelurahan) in South Jakarta. By harmonizing the GeoAI approach with robust security and privacy considerations, this research delivers a decision-support system that is analytical, trustworthy, and highly relevant for strategic planning within the MSME ecosystem. © 2026 IEEE.
