AB  - In Taiwan, tens of thousands of unregulated self-supply wells in land subsidence areas pose a significant challenge for groundwater management. Unlike manual surveys that require substantial human and time resources, artificial intelligence-based computer vision technology offers the potential for automated detection of private wells. However, given a vast number of images that must be processed during large-scale surveys, the accuracy of computer vision in well identification directly affects its practical effectiveness. To enhance the applicability of computer vision models, we analyzed the improvements from three aspects: model complexity, data diversification and balance, and ensemble decision strategies. We applied the strategies on Huwei Township in Yunlin County Taiwan. We selected four road segments with different landscape features in Beixi Village as test cases, and demonstrated the effectiveness of large-scale well spatial density estimation using Xinxing Village as an example. The results show that incorporating diversified landscape images into training data achieved validation accuracies above 90% for all five computer vision models. Across four test road segments, the addition of diversified urban landscape street view images to models initially trained solely on farmland landscape street view images significantly improved all five computer vision models, with an average accuracy increase of 24.75%. In doing so, our study comprehensively provides strategies for improving computer vision models, providing a valuable reference for detecting unregulated self-supplies in other land subsidence areas worldwide. © 2026,  Taiwan Joint Irrigation Associations. All rights reserved.
