AB  - This study uses geospatial AI (GeoAI) methods to explore how local market conditions, competition dynamics and digital infrastructure jointly influence retail location optimality. The study uses the context of refurbished mobiles, to study GeoAI derived opportunity zones for an efficient entry in the United Kingdom (UK) market. The study uses open data from 361 local authority districts (LADs) to operationalize an opportunity index at LAD level by integrating socio-economic, demographic and connectivity (digital infrastructure) measures. The raw data was made analysis ready using geospatial data engineering (using spatial joins, coordinate system harmonization and format conversion). Ensemble of local indicators of spatial resolution (LISA) and geographically weighted regression (GWR) acted as an intelligent system (GeoAI) for revealing patterns invisible to a-spatial techniques. Finding reveal positive spatial autocorrelation in opportunity, spatial heterogeneity influenced by socio-demographics and digital infrastructure and competition effects varying in magnitude across spatial regimes thereby extending location theory and offering actional intelligence for circular economy retailers navigating the tension between opportunity and competition intensity. © 2026 Elsevier Inc.
