TI  - Evaluating rooftop and ground solar photovoltaic benefits in southern Taiwan via UAV and geospatial AI
PY  - 2026
T2  - Solar Energy
VL  - 308
C7  - 114426
DO  - 10.1016/j.solener.2026.114426
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105029757676?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Civil Engineering, Republic of China Military Academy, No. 1, Weiwu Rd., Kaohsiung, Fengshan, 83059, Taiwan
AB  - Solar photovoltaic (PV) systems are crucial for mitigating climate change, yet their effective deployment in tropical regions is often constrained by building morphology, environmental conditions, and spatial heterogeneity. This study aims to develop and validate an integrated unmanned aerial vehicle and Geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI) framework for assessing rooftop and ground solar PV potential in southern Taiwan, thereby supporting sustainable building retrofitting and renewable energy planning. High-resolution imagery captured by UAVs was processed in a geographic information system (GIS) to generate orthophotos, digital surface models, slope, and aspect maps. A solar radiation model was then applied, and the deep learning–based Text SAM was employed to extract urban rooftop areas for PV installation. Rooftops were screened according to slope ('45°), aspect (non-north), radiation ('800 kWh/m2), and area ('30 m2). Photovoltaic benefits (PB) were estimated using a module efficiency of 21.6% and a performance ratio of 83%. The results show that 76 rooftops in Kaohsiung City can provide a total PB of 1,215.83 MWh/year, equivalent to approximately 10% of local household demand. Validation using a rural PV ground system in Pingtung County demonstrated only a 6.47% deviation between estimated and measured generation, confirming the reliability of the proposed method. The results can inform building retrofitting strategies that enhance energy efficiency and integrate rooftop PV into building energy management systems. This research highlights the originality of combining UAV derived datasets with GeoAI for building PV assessment, offering implications for sustainable retrofitting, intelligent energy management, and the long-term operation of the built environment. © 2026 International Solar Energy Society.
