AB  - Contamination from intensive agricultural practices, especially nitrate pollution, is posing a growing threat to groundwater supplies in arid and semi-arid countries. For groundwater management to be sustainable, vulnerable aquifer zones must be effectively identified. This study assesses groundwater vulnerability to nitrate contamination in the Maknessy Basin, central Tunisia, by integrating conventional index-based methods with advanced Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (Geo-AI) approaches. An Intrinsic Vulnerability Index (IVI) was first developed using four hydrogeological factors: groundwater depth, precipitation, vadose zone lithology, and slope. This index was subsequently enhanced by incorporating nitrate concentration and land-use data to derive a Specific Vulnerability Index (SVI), allowing for a more realistic representation of local pollution pressures. In parallel, several machine learning models, including Gradient Boosting Machine (GBM), Support Vector Machine (SVM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Random Decision Forest (RDF), Classification and Regression Trees (C&RT), and Linear Regression (LR), were applied to predict SVI values and evaluate their performance. Model accuracy was assessed using standard statistical indicators, including mean absolute error, root mean square error, and the coefficient of determination. Results indicate that the GBM model outperformed all other approaches, achieving the lowest prediction errors and the highest explanatory power, and accurately reproducing observed SVI patterns. The findings highlight the value of Geo-AI in enhancing groundwater vulnerability assessment and highlight the shortcomings of conventional vulnerability models in capturing intricate non-linear relationships. In arid and semi-arid regions, this integrated framework offers a strong decision-support tool for sustainable resource management and groundwater preservation. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
