UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046070184?origin=resultslist
AD  - Politehnica University Timişoara, Faculty of Electronics, Telecommunications and Information Technologies, Communication Department, Timişoara, Romania
AB  - This work presents an urban digital twin for Timioara, Romania built from fragmented public datasets in the absence of official 3D city models. The implementation integrates municipal CKAN resources, OpenStreetMap extracts, and Copernicus elevation data through adapter-based ingestion with enforced schemas and automated versioning. Three technical challenges motivated the design: heterogeneous coordinate reference systems (Stereo 70, WGS84, UTM Zone 34N) requiring canonical reprojection, conflicting update cadences across daily OSM snapshots and static municipal layers, and inconsistent geometry validity requiring automated repair workflows. Vector embeddings enable semantic search over dataset metadata; Precision@5 improves from 0.46 to 0.74 compared to keyword approaches, with P95 query latency under 510ms. Blockchain Merkle roots anchor data provenance while keeping geospatial files off-chain; tamper detection achieved 100% accuracy across 400 deliberate corruption trials. Ten repeated builds achieved identical hashes with 98.5% resource retrieval success, completing in 46 minutes with 2.8% cryptographic overhead. The system generates a 2.5D representation pairing OSM building footprints with inferred heights from Copernicus DEM and building-level attributes. The results demonstrate that operational urban twins can be assembled from incomplete, multi-authority sources when paired with strict version control, semantic discovery, and cryptographic integrity verification, enabling municipal stakeholders to query integrated infrastructure data reliably. © 2026 IEEE.
