AD  - School of Environment Science and Spatial Informatics, China University of Mining and Technology, No. 1 Daxue Road, Xuzhou, 221116, China
AB  - Using tool sequences for tackling geospatial tasks is a challenge. While large language models (LLMs) excel in generating these sequences, their computational costs restrict deployment. Small language models (SLMs) offer an alternative. Traditional approaches directly train SLMs to generate tool sequences often fail due to entangled reasoning paths and excessive load. Therefore, we propose MiniGeoSolver, a progressive training strategy designed to enhance SLMs. To establish a clear reasoning trajectory, MiniGeoSolver breaks down the complex sequences generation process into two stages. First, it constructs a symbolic framework to determine required tools. Then, it aligns detailed execution with this framework to generate accurate workflows. We fine-tune Gemma-2-2B using a split dataset and use an evaluation dataset to assess task-solving ability. Results demonstrate that our model outperforms GPT-4 by 22.7% and Gemini 1.5 Pro by 17.2%. Additionally, our progressive strategy realizes 9.5% higher accuracy than data generated without a progressive design. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
