AB  - Deepfake satellite images pose a significant challenge to geospatial science, necessitating robust detection methods tailored to the unique characteristics of remote sensing data. In this study, we investigate two key aspects that have been overlooked in the emerging literature on satellite imagery deepfake detection: the impact of finetuning classifiers on domain-specific remote sensing datasets, and the use of frequency-domain classifiers. Our findings show that finetuning spatial-domain classifiers significantly enhances both accuracy and robustness, while frequency-domain classifiers offer superior baseline performance and consistency. By combining the strengths of both domains, an ensemble model achieves the highest accuracy (99.65%) and demonstrates resilience across a range of perturbations, outperforming individual classifiers. These results underscore the importance of domain-specific finetuning and ensemble methods for advancing deepfake satellite image detection. © 2025 IEEE.
