AB  - With the rapid advancement in remote sensing (RS) technology, a huge amount of Earth observation (EO) data featuring considerable and complicated heterogeneity is readily available nowadays, which allows researchers to tackle current geoscience applications freshly. It is sensible to leverage the complementary information of different sensors to enhance the spatial-spectral resolution and further improve the accuracy and reliability of data. On this account, multimodal RS data fusion has experienced tremendous progress. Particularly, owing to impressive ability of deep neural networks in feature representation and data reconstruction, deep multimodal RS data fusion has made remarkable advancements, enabling us to obtain a more comprehensive and accurate understanding of the Earth's surface and supporting various applications in environmental monitoring, resource management, disaster response, and beyond. This chapter aims to present a systematic overview of multi-modal deep learning for Geospatial Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI). Concretely, we first give a brief review of DL-based multimodal RS data fusion. Then, from the perspectives of homogeneous data fusion and heterogeneous data fusion, we comprehensively summarize the deep fusion methods based on different deep learning technologies, e.g., spectral pansharpening, spatiospectral fusion, spatiotemporal fusion, radar-optical fusion, synthetic aperture radar-optical, and RS-Geospatial Big Data. Specifically, in each sub-field, the remaining challenges and potential future directions are highlighted. © 2024 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
