AD  - Department of Geodesy Engineering, Institut Teknologi Nasional Malang, Malang, Indonesia
AB  - Efficient irrigation infrastructure management is essential for achieving sustainable water distribution and agricultural productivity. This study presents the development of an Industry Foundation Classes (IFC)-based Building Information Modeling (BIM) framework integrated with CesiumJS to enable a web-based GeoBIM visualization system for the Pataruman Weir, Indonesia. Multi-sensor data acquisition—including Terrestrial Laser Scanning (TLS), UAV LiDAR, and handheld LiDAR—was conducted to achieve a geometrically accurate three-dimensional reconstruction, with a Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of 0.032 m. The BIM model was semantically enriched according to the Ministry of Public Works and Housing Regulation No. 23/PRT/M/2015 and exported in IFC format to ensure OpenBIM interoperability. The model was then converted to 3D Tiles and deployed on CesiumJS for browser-based interaction. The resulting WebBIM system, named DAM2TWIN, integrates BIM semantics, spatial context, and Digital Terrain Models (DTMs) within a single platform, enabling visualization, spatial querying, and attribute inspection directly through the web. Performance evaluation demonstrated efficient rendering and data loading, validating the system’s capability to handle high-fidelity BIM models in lightweight web environments. This study highlights the potential of open GeoBIM workflows for improving transparency, interoperability, and data-driven decision-making in irrigation asset management, while providing a scalable foundation for future GeoAI and Internet of Things (IoT)-based digital twin applications. © 2025 IEEE.
