AB  - Urban food demand is shaped by complex spatiotemporal interactions among weather conditions, time-of-day, and cultural events. This paper proposes a practical AI-driven framework that integrates remote sensing-derived meteorological data, temporal context, and calendar features to predict hourly restaurant demand (PAX) in an Indian metropolitan setting. The framework fuses structured data sources - weather (temperature, humidity, rainfall, wind), time, and event indicators - through machine learning models including SVR, XGBoost, and LSTM, combined via a lightweight ensemble. Experiments on 13,864 restaurant transactions and 1,538 hourly weather observations from Mumbai show an ensemble performance of RMSE = 13.84, MAE =1 3. 9 0, and R2=0.743 on a held-out test set. Geospatial visualization is presented as an exploratory module and is not quantitatively benchmarked. The work establishes a transferable, climate-aware demand forecasting pipeline to support staffing, inventory, and delivery planning under weather and event variability. © 2025 IEEE.
