AB  - The surge in geospatial data intensifies geoprivacy protection challenges, spanning methodological issues, cultural and regulatory contexts, and public attitudes, necessitating effective frameworks to ensure privacy and ethical data use. This study's importance lies in addressing these complexities to promote resilient data practices globally. Using a mixed-method approach, we analyzed 526 Web of Science articles (2008–2024) and surveyed 4260 participants across six study areas (Hong Kong, Japan, New Zealand, South Korea, the United Kingdom, and the United States). The bibliometric analysis traces methodological shifts from basic privacy techniques to AI-driven solutions, while the survey reveals diverse public attitudes shaped by cultural and regulatory factors. Innovatively, this research integrates these dimensions, proposing adaptive, context-sensitive geoprivacy strategies—such as GeoAI-enhanced differential privacy and layered privacy models—to balance utility and protection. These solutions enhance privacy governance by accommodating global disparities, offering practical pathways for policymakers and researchers to navigate geoprivacy challenges effectively. © 2025 The Author(s). Transactions in GIS published by John Wiley & Sons Ltd.
