AB  - The need for this work is more important today, which assists in maintaining high efficiency while maximizing bifacial PVs' energy harvest from solar under varying environmental conditions since they offer energy generation capability from both sides. However, in the cases of current designs, the installation or passive adjustment in the angle tilt does not sufficiently capture variable solar irradiation, ground albedo, and real-time meteorological conditions. Such AI-based works today only represent a single-agent system where feedback from some physical domain such as thermal response as well as radiative dynamics is not integrated, thus limiting the generalizability and operational accuracy sets of such models. Here, a complete novel AI-based multi-method adaptive control framework for dynamic tilt optimization in bifacial PV arrays is introduced. At the heart of the system is a Hierarchical DualAgent Reinforcement Learning with Radiance-Augmented Feedback (HDARL-RAF), which gives rise to real-time tilt angle adjustments fine-tuned through multi-directional irradiance along bifacial gain feedback. An Adaptive Multivariate Entropy-Based Forecasting and Control (AMEFC) model addresses the uncertainty dimension in forecasting by adding entropy-weighted LSTM layers designed to make tilt more predictable and robust in performance. A Bidirectional OpticalThermal Coupled Simulation Engine (BOTCSE) was established for validation of the radiative and thermal outputs during tilt variations. For geo-optimizing these models, the Geospatial-AI Fusion Mapping with Tilt Sensitivity Indexing (GAFM-TSI) creates local tilt models through irradiance and terrain data samples derived from satellites. Lastly, a Hybrid Physics Informed Deep Surrogate Model for Tilt Control (HPDSM-TC) enables lightweight, physics-guided control deployment on embedded systems. Thus, when all put together, they can deliver up to 24% bifacial gain, a reduction of 15% of forecast error, and an 11.8% increase in thermal-optical efficiency, all this while ensuring low-latency and scalable deployment. Overall, this research makes a significant step in integrating intelligent control with physics-informed validation across environmental and geographic domains in intelligent solar adaptive harvesting configuration sets. © 2025 IEEE.
