AD  - Department of Geography, University of Colorado Boulder, Boulder, CO, United States
AB  - Postdisaster damage assessment is crucial for timely response and effective recovery. However, traditional methods relying on extensive field reconnaissance are inherently slow, resource-intensive, and often inadequate for the scale and urgency demanded by modern disasters. Earth observation (EO), particularly remote sensing imagery, offers a powerful alternative for rapid and synoptic damage mapping. This chapter provides a review of the progress of machine learning (ML) and geospatial artificial intelligence in unlocking the potential of remote sensing images for damage assessments. We demonstrate how integrating advanced ML with high-resolution satellite imagery can enable automated, rapid, and scalable damage assessment and monitoring of postdisaster recovery. The chapter includes illustrative case studies highlighting the practical application and benefits of this integrated approach. We further discuss challenges and future directions in this integration for significantly improved disaster response capabilities. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved..
