AD  - University of Melbourne, Melbourne, Australia
AB  - Recent urban flood disasters and climate uncertainties underscore the urgency of advanced flood risk assessments in urban areas, considering the growing flooding risks to critical urban infrastructure, ecosystems, and communities. Brisbane is selected as a case study due to its frequent historical flood events and its significance as the host city for the 2032 Olympic Games - necessitating flood resilience a critical planning priority. This study employs geospatial analysis with a Random Forest (RF) machine learning model to map flood susceptibility, using six key input variables: digital elevation model (DEM), slope, soil permeability, distance to water area, distance to sea, and historic flood. Spatial data were processed using ArcGIS Pro, and the RF model was implemented in R with a training/testing split to enable robust performance evaluation. The RF model achieved strong predictive performance, with an Area Under the ROC Curve (AUC) of 0.946 and overall accuracy exceeding 86%. The resulting flood susceptibility map identifies high-risk zones concentrated near major rivers (e.g., along the Brisbane River) and in low-elevation suburban areas. This RF-based approach provides a valuable tool to support urban resilience, informing targeted flood mitigation strategies and adaptation planning in Brisbane. © 2025, The Architectural Science Association and The University of Melbourne, Australia.
