AD  - Metropolitan Electricity Authority, Geo-Information on Distribution System Office, Bangkok, Thailand
AB  - Thailand's solar market is rapidly growing, driven by a favorable geographical location, abundant sunlight, supportive governmental policies, decreasing installation costs, and reduced consumer electricity bills. Metropolitan Electricity Authority (MEA) is an electrical provider in the metropolitan region. MEA actively supports government policies regarding renewable energy to support electricity users installing solar systems. Solar panel installation needs to comply with MEA regulations and safety standards. However, the issue arises from unregistered on-grid solar systems connected to electricity systems. These unauthorized connections pose a danger to MEA's operating officers and can cause damage to electrical equipment from excess electrical power.To solve this issue, MEA has initiated a project to investigate unregistered on-grid solar installations. As solar installations continue to increase, traditional field inspections and manual image reviews have become increasingly time-consuming, requiring 40 personnel and approximately 30 days per cycle. Despite these efforts, installation targets remain unmet. This study introduces a GeoAI workflow that integrates deep learning-based solar panel detection with GIS-enabled spatial analysis. The framework leverages high-resolution drone imagery (5 cm GSD) and combines transfer learning with custom model development. A fine-tuned Mask R-CNN model achieved an F1-score of 0.864, demonstrating the effectiveness of leveraging existing domain knowledge for rapid deployment in utility operations. Compared to traditional methods, this approach resulted in a 100% reduction in workforce requirements due to the fully automated workflow.Moreover, the deep learning approach reduces processing time by approximately 85% and eliminates related labor costs, enabling repeatable and large-scale monitoring. These results show that deep learning and GeoAI technologies can enhance operational efficiency, reduce costs, and support electricity providers in managing the increasing adoption of solar panel systems. © 2025 IEEE.
