AB  - The past decade has seen rapid integration of artificial intelligence (AI) in welfare and emergency response domains, enabling novel capabilities in incident detection, situational awareness, and resource optimization. However, existing AI-driven systems remain highly fragmented by domain and function, with isolated tools for tasks like fall detection in elder care, wildlife rescue, or emergency dispatch. Consequently, critical delays and inefficiencies persist, especially in under-served or rural regions. For instance, studies show rural pre-hospital response times remain significantly longer than urban counterparts, and even well-developed AI classifiers for emergency call data achieve only marginal gains (approximately 93% accuracy). This survey reviews the state of the art (2020–2025) in AI-enabled welfare and emergency platforms, categorizing them by architecture (vision-based, GeoAI, decision support, crowdsourced/mobile) and application domain (elderly care, animal rescue, food security, etc.). We highlight key technical and ethical gapsnotably the absence of a cross-domain, geospatiallyaware, decentralized coordination framework. Finally, we argue for a unified, centralized platform, such as the proposed Centralized Aid and Response Platform (CARP), that integrates AI with real-time geospatial intelligence and interoperable services. Such a platform could substantially reduce wasted resources from siloed solutions and accelerate life-saving responses from technical, societal, and operational standpoints. © Grenze Scientific Society, 2025.
