TI  - Evolution and Drivers of Wetland Types in China's Coastal Wetlands (1990-2020)
PY  - 2025
T2  - International Conference on Geoinformatics
DO  - 10.1109/Geoinformatics67279.2025.11173525
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105018744829?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Surveying and Geo-Informatics, Tongji University, Shanghai, 200092, China
AD  - School of Resource and Environmental Sciences, Wuhan University, Hubei, Wuhan, China
AD  - Northwest Institute of Eco-Environment and Resources, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Lanzhou, China
AB  - China's coastal wetlands are ecologically rich but lack long-term, high-resolution maps, impeding research on wetland change drivers. To address this, we developed a 1990-2020 wetland dataset using Google Earth Engine (GEE) and geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI), based on Landsat imagery (30 -meter resolution), to analyze spatiotemporal dynamics and driving forces across 12 coastal provinces (Liaoning, Hebei, Tianjin, Shandong, Jiangsu, Shanghai, Zhejiang, Fujian, Guangdong, Guangxi, Taiwan, Hainan). Results show a total wetland area decline of approximately 8.67 %, from 61,932 km2 to 56,543 km2. High-value wetlands (e.g., rivers, lakes, tidal flats) decreased by 22.45 %, while medium-value wetlands (e.g., agricultural ponds, coastal breeding ponds) increased by 46.27 %. Non-wetland areas (e.g., forests, built-up land) were slightly reduced by 1.03 %, though impervious surfaces surged by 150.28 %. Wetland changes followed three main patterns: stability, primarily in ecologically protected areas (e.g., Fujian, Hainan); degradation, driven by urbanization, industrialization, and agriculture (e.g., Jiangsu, Zhejiang); and limited recovery, such as through mangrove planting. Key direct drivers include urbanization converting wetlands to built-up areas, agricultural expansion encroaching on high-value wetlands, and climate change (e.g., sea-level rise) affecting stability. Indirect drivers encompass policy interventions (e.g., wetland protection laws) promoting stability and recovery, economic growth intensifying degradation, and technological advances enhancing monitoring capabilities. These findings highlight pressing challenges for coastal wetland conservation, emphasizing the need for integrated policies that balance ecological protection and economic development. By aligning with global biodiversity and sustainability frameworks, this study provides actionable evidence to guide national and regional policy-making and advance the achievement of SDG targets.  © 2025 IEEE.
