TI  - Particulate Matter Pollution and Pediatric Renal Health: Insights from a Study on Chronic Kidney Disease in Children
PY  - 2024
T2  - 18th Conference of the International Society of Indoor Air Quality and Climate, INDOOR AIR 2024 - Conference Program and Proceedings
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85210803496?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Environmental and Occupational Health, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, 704, Taiwan
AD  - Research Center of Environmental Trace Toxic Substances, College of Medicine, National Cheng Kung University, Tainan, 704, Taiwan
AD  - Department of Environmental and Occupational Medicine, National Taiwan University Hospital Yunlin Branch, Yunlin, Taiwan
AD  - Department of Pediatrics, National Cheng Kung University Hospital, Tainan, Taiwan
AB  - In the context of air pollution, smaller particles of ambient particulate matter (PM) can pose heightened toxicity as they penetrate deeply into the terminal bronchioles and alveoli. Children are particularly sensitive to PM air pollution due to its adverse health effects on renal function. Chronic kidney disease (CKD) in children presents a significant health challenge, potentially leading to renal failure and associated complications. Despite this, uncertainties persist regarding the specific impact of smaller PM on childhood kidney health. Limited evidence exists on the influence of PM on pediatric renal function, particularly in indoor environments where children spend a substantial portion of their time. This study explores the intricate relationship between PM exposure and the decline in chronic renal function in children aged 2-18 years. Enrolling 73 children with CKD and 32 in a control group in Southern Taiwan from 08/2021 to 09/2023, the research examines blood and urine samples for renal function, inflammatory biomarkers (IF), and oxidative stress indicators (OS). The study compares the effects on children's kidneys from PM with aerodynamic diameters of ≤1 μm (PM1), ≤2.5 μm (PM2.5), and ≤10 μm (PM10), respectively. Considering that children spend most of their time in their bedrooms, followed by school and the living room, personal exposure to daily PM concentration based on their activities is estimated by the Geo-AI predictive model. This model utilizes monitoring air quality station data, meteorological information, land-use features, elevation, pollutant levels, indoor environmental parameters, human activities, and real-time measurements (R2 > 0.80). The Mann-Whitney U test is employed to reveal the relationship between different PM sizes and renal function, IF, and OS. A Generalized Additive Model estimates changes in estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) levels with each 10 μg/m3 increase in PM, adjusting for age, sex, season, and body mass index. The findings disclose a negative correlation between PM exposure and eGFR in children with CKD, accompanied by a positive association with the inflammatory marker TNFR2. Additionally, PM exposure is linked to a decrease in urine epidermal growth factor (EGF), indicating compromised renal tubular health. In terms of the single impact on eGFR, elevated concentrations of PM1, PM2.5, and PM10 (each increment of 10 μg/m3) were associated with a reduction in eGFR. Moreover, compared with the effects of PM2.5, and PM10, the effects of PM1 appear to be more pronounced in specific subgroups, including boys and girls, and children aged 4 years or younger. Furthermore, PM effects were more pronounced during polluted seasons. In summary, this study highlights that exposure to particulate matter, especially smaller PM1, may detrimentally impact eGFR, induce renal inflammation, and contribute to renal damage in children with CKD. © 2024 18th Conference of the International Society of Indoor Air Quality and Climate, INDOOR AIR 2024 - Conference Program and Proceedings. All rights reserved.
