TI  - Deep Learning-Based Wildfire Risk Prediction with Scenario-Specific Model Recommendations
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE 16th Annual Ubiquitous Computing, Electronics and Mobile Communication Conference, UEMCON 2025
SP  - 434
EP  - 440
DO  - 10.1109/UEMCON67449.2025.11267639
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105031446844?origin=resultslist
AD  - Meharry Medical College, Department of Computer Science and Data Science, TN, United States
AB  - Wildfires are a growing global threat with severe environmental, economic, and human losses. Early and accurate detection is critical for reducing damage and enabling a timely response. Recent advances in deep learning, especially convolutional neural networks (CNNs), have shown strong potential in image-based wildfire detection. However, most existing studies focus on single-model solutions without considering different operational scenarios. This limits the practical deployment of such systems across diverse environments, from low-power edge devices to high-capacity centralized monitoring centers. In this study, we compare four CNN models, SimpleCNN, MobileNetV2, EfficientNetB0, and ResNet50V2, for wildfire risk prediction using a curated dataset of fire and non-fire images. We analyze performance for each model and map it to the most suitable deployment scenarios. With 91.2 % accuracy and 0.913 F1-score, EfficientNetB0 was the best balanced model for general-purpose applications. MobileNetV2 excelled in high-precision edge deployments with 90.5 % accuracy and a 0.902 F1-score. ResNet50V2 had the greatest recall for high-risk central monitoring situations with a 91.0 % accuracy and a 0.908 F1-score. SimpleCNN is viable for resource-constrained hardware despite its lower accuracy of 87.3 % and F1-score of 0.872. Our results show that EfficientNetB0 gives the best all-around performance, MobileNetV2 works best for high-precision edge applications, ResNet50V2 achieves the highest recall for high-risk central monitoring, and SimpleCNN is a good choice for very limited hardware. These findings demonstrate that no single model is optimal for all wildfire monitoring conditions. Matching the right model to the right placement setting improves both accuracy and efficiency. This scenario-based approach provides a practical way to apply AI-driven wildfire risk prediction across different environments. © 2025 IEEE.
