AD  - Department of Civil, Materials, and Environmental Engineering, University of Illinois Chicago, 2095 Engineering Research Facility, 842 W. Taylor St., Chicago, 60607, IL, United States
AB  - Shared e-scooters are widely adopted in U.S. cities. Understanding the determinants of demand, especially the influence of the built environment and spatially varying factors, is essential to enhancing system efficiency. Using data across census tracts in Chicago, this study compares multiscale geographically weighted regression (MGWR) and the Spatial Lag Model (SLM) with tree-based machine learning models (Random Forest, XGBoost, and LightGBM) to capture nonlinear and spatially non-stationary relationships between the built environment, sociodemographic factors, and shared e-scooter trip patterns. We applied an AutoML approach to optimize model selection and performance. A Geo-XAI (geospatial explainable AI) framework that integrates machine learning and the GeoShapley method is then employed to interpret spatially heterogeneous effects. Results show that demand is highest in transit-rich, amenity-dense neighborhoods. Key predictors include transit accessibility, the proportion of young adults aged 18–34, and educational attainment, all of which have strong positive effects on shared e-scooter use. GeoShapley analysis highlights spatial variability in these effects, and transit accessibility exerts the strongest influence in central neighborhoods. Job entropy and the share of zero-vehicle households also show positive but smaller associations, while network density has minimal impact. The study provides insights into where and why shared e-scooter demand concentrates and helps decision-makers develop strategies that optimize fleet allocation and infrastructure investment. Copyright © 2025. Published by Elsevier B.V.
