AB  - Air pollution, exacerbated by both anthropogenic and natural factors, presents a significant global threat to environmental and public health. This chapter aims to elucidate the development of a geospatial artificial intelligence (geo-AI) model designed to accurately estimate air pollution concentrations. The model integrates leave-one-out cross-validation (LOOCV) kriging land use regression with machine learning–based algorithms, incorporating diverse factors such as land cover, land use inventories, road networks, industrial activities, sociodemographic profiles, meteorological conditions, and topographical features as predictive variables. The proposed geo-AI model demonstrates high accuracy in estimating air pollution concentrations, supported by robust explanatory power. By assessing the relative contributions of pollutant-related predictors, the model identifies key variables influencing emissions. Rigorous validation tests confirm the model reliability. Practically, the spatiotemporal estimates generated by the model effectively capture variations in daily pollution levels and offer valuable insights for evaluating associated health outcomes. This framework represents a significant advancement in assessing air quality at both local and regional scales. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
