AD  - Department of Geography, University of Wisconsin-Madison, Madison, WI, United States
AB  - In this chapter, we discuss the critical issue of trajectory privacy protection in the era of widespread location-based services (LBS). With the growing availability of individual-level trajectory data-derived from mobile devices, global positioning system (GPS) units, and geotagged social media-ensuring geoprivacy remains a paramount concern. We first review four primary privacy protection frameworks-de-identification, geomasking, K-anonymity, and differential privacy-emphasizing the persistent challenges they face in balancing privacy and data utility, handling data sparsity, and maintaining representativeness. To exemplify practical solutions, we present a case study using vehicle trajectory data. We introduce a privacy-preserving Vehicle Trajectory Simulation and Visualization (VTSV) framework that integrates geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI) and differential privacy. This framework applies differential privacy techniques to origin-destination data, ensuring user anonymity, and employs route planning and deep reinforcement learning-based driving simulations to produce high-resolution, behavior-customized synthetic trajectories. These trajectories preserve critical spatiotemporal patterns without compromising individual privacy. The results highlight that VTSV effectively mitigates privacy risks, enabling safer data sharing and analysis while retaining essential insights about human mobility. This work underscores the importance of developing robust and adaptive privacy-preserving methods that support innovative urban mobility research and applications. © 2025 Xiao Huang, Xinyue Ye, Kathleen Stewart, and Subasish Das. All rights reserved.
