AB  - Persistent cloud coverage exceeding 70% annually severely restricts optical remote sensing in tropical regions, creating critical gaps in operational land cover mapping. This research addresses this limitation by systematically comparing six image enhancement methods that transform grayscale Interferometric Synthetic Aperture Radar (IFSAR) data into interpretable Color Orthorectified Radar Imagery (CORI) suitable for automated classification. We evaluated CycleGAN-based translation against five pansharpening techniques (Gram-Schmidt, Wavelet, IHS, Brovey, PCA) using both image quality metrics and U-Net semantic segmentation performance across vegetation, bareland, and waterbody classes in Alaska's boreal landscape. Results reveal that Wavelet pansharpening and CycleGAN both enable effective automated classification, achieving 0.81-0.90 mIoU and 0.67-0.83 mIoU respectively-approaching performance levels of true optical imagery. Critically, traditional image quality metrics (RMSE, CC, SAM) don't reliably predict classification accuracy; CycleGAN's low correlation coefficient (0.35) didn't prevent strong segmentation performance, while PCA's moderate quality scores corresponded to poor classification results. The methods differ fundamentally in deployment: Wavelet offers immediate operational implementation without training requirements, while CycleGAN provides essential scalability across sensors, resolutions, and classification taxonomies through transfer learning. These findings validate CORI as an operationally viable solution for national mapping agencies in cloud-covered regions. By combining IFSAR's all-weather acquisition capability with color-based interpretability, the validated workflows enable continuous land cover monitoring, environmental assessment, and disaster response when persistent cloud cover eliminates optical alternatives. The research provides practical methodologies for tropical mapping programs facing weather-imposed data acquisition constraints. © ACRS 2025.All rights reserved.
