AB  - Childhood growth faltering remains a public-health challenge in Indonesia, yet risk mapping is still dominated by coarse administrative summaries that obscure local heterogeneity. This paper introduces a lightweight Geo-AI pipeline that merges routinely collected public health records with spatial and socio-economic layers to generate fine-scale risk surfaces for Tasikmalaya City. After basic cleaning and feature engineering, 1824 case points (2020-2023) from 27 health centres were modelled using a two-layer Multi-Layer Perceptron (32-32-1, ReLU, 0.20 dropout). The network achieved an in-sample mean absolute error of 6.9 ± 3.1 cases and explained 87 ± 6% of inter-annual variance across 92 facility-year combinations. Predictions are served through a Streamlit-Folium dashboard that allows users to filter years, overlay covariates, and query individual facilities, enabling rapid identification of high-risk clusters. While forecast validation with chronological hold-outs is still required, the platform already demonstrates how inexpensive edge-deployable models can enrich city-level nutrition planning with evidence-driven, sub-district insight.  © 2025 IEEE.
