AD  - University of Basilicata, Laboratory of Urban and Regional System Engineering (LISUT), School of Engineering, Potenza, Italy
AB  - Metrology contributes significantly to territorial planning. The possibility of measuring several components and dynamics insisting on a territory allows for the validation of choices for the sustainable development of a context. This paper discusses the role of metrology in spatial planning and the importance of precise measurement and data interpretation in making informed decisions. The increasing availability of geospatial data has led to the development of a new trend in geospatial computing, geoAI, which uses interdisciplinary methods, ranging from computer science, statistics and geography, to optimize the analysis of spatial dynamics. Accurate measurement also ensure that the various components of a given area are properly analyzed and understood. The aim is to make planning choices targeting spatial development most akin to places and maximize positive impacts on territory. However, selecting the correct statistical unit for analysis is still a challenge, and the modifiable areal unit problem (MAUP) can affect the insights gained from data analysis. The focus of this contribution is to indicate a validation metric for regionalization issue using techniques from information theory, such as the Kullback-Leibler Divergence (KL). Specifically, a normalized and symmetrical version of the KL divergence is used to identify a proper region subdivision and the appropriate set of parameters to use. The key idea is to validate the regionalization by maximizing the divergence across the analyzed parameter set. Further, identify the parameters with the highest variability that can contribute to validate the chosen region subdivision. © 2023 IEEE.
