AD  - School of Geographical Sciences and Urban Planning, Arizona State University, United States
AB  - GeoAI is gaining an increasingly prominent place in the geographic research agenda. Researchers now regularly use GeoAI to study a range of social and environmental problems, and in some areas of research GeoAI methods appear to be becoming the approach of choice. However, as the number of GeoAI studies has expanded, less attention has been devoted to how GeoAI fits within the scientific traditions of the field of Geography. While many researchers using GeoAI frame their work within the context of a Fourth Paradigm for scientific exploration, those same researchers rarely explicitly discuss how that work relates to core principles of scientific discovery. We begin to address this issue by examining how the reproducibility and replicability of research relates to the development and use of GeoAI. To introduce the topic, we define reproductions and replications in the context of GeoAI research. We then offer guidance for researchers interested in enhancing the reproducibility of their GeoAI studies, giving particular attention to some of the unique challenges presented when studying phenomena using spatial data and GeoAI. Looking to the future, we present several lines of reproduction and replication related inquiry that researchers could pursue to quicken the development of GeoAI. © 2024 selection and editorial matter, Song Gao, Yingjie Hu, and Wenwen Li; individual chapters, the contributors.
